WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:05.948
Demo four, part one of three. What changed since demo three, and
where Factory goes.

2
00:00:11.500 --> 00:00:17.188
Blue means live. Purple means a recorded real run. Red means
scripted, with no model.

3
00:00:18.000 --> 00:00:26.088
Teal means a decision that the AI Simulated User takes at the
owner's request. Grey means that waiting time is cut inside one
page.

4
00:00:28.100 --> 00:00:37.124
The AI Simulated User operates Factory at the owner's request.
No human appears in this demo, and every review is an agent
review.

5
00:00:42.333 --> 00:00:48.861
This is the path of one change through Factory. Grey steps did
not change since demo three. Blue steps did.

6
00:00:50.833 --> 00:00:57.985
Repository onboarding: a scripted setup agent proposes the setup
files, and Factory checks them. Fake only.

7
00:00:58.285 --> 00:01:03.157
Triage offers three suggested answers for each open question.
Real.

8
00:01:03.457 --> 00:01:10.753
QA tests: each failing test has a reason, and each test is
reviewed against its requirement. Fake only.

9
00:01:11.053 --> 00:01:17.077
The Judge loop scores the candidate, and starts a revision
inside approved limits. Real.

10
00:01:17.433 --> 00:01:23.817
Review: without evidence for a human check, it stays pending,
with notes. Offline checks.

11
00:01:24.117 --> 00:01:33.021
Across every step: simpler pages, with a new menu, Needs you,
Work items, Run, and Review. Offline checks.

12
00:01:33.321 --> 00:01:39.465
Work items with parts: one by default, split only after a
deployability review. Real.

13
00:01:39.765 --> 00:01:43.029
Next, each blue step, with a real screen.

14
00:01:46.133 --> 00:01:53.933
Repository onboarding. A scripted setup agent proposes the setup
files, and Factory checks them. Status: fake only.

15
00:01:54.733 --> 00:01:58.861
Here is the real page. Every proposed command is marked Not run.

16
00:02:02.533 --> 00:02:09.709
Triage. Each open question has three suggested answers, and
there is one clear next step. Status: real.

17
00:02:11.133 --> 00:02:15.789
Here is a real conversation: the questions, and the answers that
were chosen.

18
00:02:19.400 --> 00:02:28.280
QA tests. Each failing test gets a reason, and every test is
reviewed against its requirement before approval. Status: fake
only.

19
00:02:29.500 --> 00:02:34.444
Here is the real per-test review, with Matches the requirement,
and Does not match.

20
00:02:38.234 --> 00:02:46.682
The Judge loop. The Judge scores four dimensions, and a low
score starts a revision inside approved limits. Status: real.

21
00:02:47.934 --> 00:02:52.590
Here is a real loop: the round, the four scores, and the limits.

22
00:02:56.401 --> 00:03:04.777
Review. When the evidence for a human check is missing, the
review stays pending, with notes. Status: offline checks.

23
00:03:05.801 --> 00:03:11.177
Here is a real review: the checks that have no evidence yet,
with their saved notes.

24
00:03:15.734 --> 00:03:23.390
Work items with parts. One work item by default, and a split
only after a deployability review. Status: real.

25
00:03:25.134 --> 00:03:28.110
Here is a real parent page, with its parts.

26
00:03:32.267 --> 00:03:39.947
Simpler pages. A new menu, and Needs you shows one next action
for each work item. Status: offline checks.

27
00:03:41.667 --> 00:03:43.923
Here is the real Needs you page.

28
00:03:48.434 --> 00:03:56.882
Platform. Docker as a second engine with real-sandbox CI,
monorepo project scopes, and dot net eleven. Status: real.

29
00:03:57.434 --> 00:04:01.658
Here is the real Repositories page, with the container engine
line.

30
00:04:03.801 --> 00:04:08.841
The new menu. Needs you comes first, with one next action for
each work item.

31
00:04:16.001 --> 00:04:19.241
Work items lists every request with its state.

32
00:04:28.201 --> 00:04:31.969
Runs lists every attempt, with its status and cost.

33
00:04:41.768 --> 00:04:45.680
Now the direction. This is a plan, not delivered features.

34
00:04:47.468 --> 00:04:53.708
At the center is AI engineering execution. It is the core, and
it is what Factory does today.

35
00:04:55.968 --> 00:05:01.680
Next: a light business collaboration interface, connected to
existing business systems.

36
00:05:01.980 --> 00:05:09.204
Later: infrastructure-as-code authoring and review, and release
readiness, through existing delivery systems.

37
00:05:09.504 --> 00:05:18.936
Factory connects to the tools you already use: Jira, Azure
DevOps, GitHub, Terraform, and CI/CD. It does not replace them.

38
00:05:19.236 --> 00:05:27.180
The rules stay: humans approve consequential AI actions, and
budgets, execution boundaries, and evidence stay.

39
00:05:28.480 --> 00:05:33.856
What part one shows, and what it does not. The status badges
come from the status page of the day.

40
00:05:36.980 --> 00:05:44.204
The screens are real Factory pages. Fake only and offline checks
do not mean a feature was proven on a real task.

41
00:05:46.480 --> 00:05:50.680
The AI Simulated User was played by Claude Code at the owner's
request.
